From 678e4be948823d3390888d75cc1d6c16415e68ea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bryan Roscoe <bryanroscoe@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 22:50:20 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] AI: score the attack as a whole when a free-for-all is down
 to two players

When a free-for-all is down to two players, the free-for-all comparison no
longer runs and the duel's certified-lethal alpha strike is gated on a
two-player game (state.players.len() == 2), so attacks fall back to the
per-attacker verdicts, which meet every attacker with the defender's best
blocker. One untapped creature again stops a whole attack on paper, and an
attack that kills through blockers is pruned for crackback from the player
it kills.

In that endgame the attack is now re-scored as a whole, as the comparison
does (one blocker per attacker, the defender's own block heuristic). Under
Stabilize the re-scored attack is taken only if it kills the opponent. An
attack the model says kills skips crackback pruning, as PushLethal does.
DownsideWeighted attackers held back for an animatable man-land aren't added
back. Among attacks that kill, ties now go to the larger attack, for margin
(this also applies in the free-for-all comparison). Duels are unchanged.

Deck-lab traces (39 four-player precon games, Easy): with one opponent left
the AI declared 21% of the creatures offered as attackers and attacked with
nothing in 46% of decisions; the two-player endgame lasted 3.3 rounds on
average, up to 27. See commander-decks docs/lab/aitempo-2026-10-20.md.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
